#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049482 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048259 :topic/math-academy-id 1789 :topic/title "Parametric Surfaces"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048858 :topic/math-academy-id 873 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Horizontal Hyperbolas"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048918 :topic/math-academy-id 2981 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Vertical Hyperbolas"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049672 :topic/math-academy-id 2746 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Ellipses"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050123 :topic/math-academy-id 1895 :topic/title "Cylinders"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050769 :topic/math-academy-id 875 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Parabolas"}] :topic/id #uuid "f8ab0e7c-b362-506f-9178-56b4b5a5b57a" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175970 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175820 :question/id #uuid "616e03ca-b0fc-53f1-baf0-8034d7731891" :question/math-academy-id "e-8885" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 4\\sin{t}, \\: z \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y \\rangle = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 4\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\frac{16{sin}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\cos{t}}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing an Elliptic Cylinder"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175972 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175822 :question/id #uuid "3e65aff0-a768-58fd-b41c-ca38ce3eb0bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8888" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,y) = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: \\text{___} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $2x^2=z.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,2{x}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ and $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} 2x^2}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Parabolic Cylinder"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175973 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175823 :question/id #uuid "3c0c59e2-ceaa-54ab-bb56-3cb6daadb5f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8889" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,y) = \\big \\langle 4\\tan{t}, \\, y, \\, \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad t\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,y)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\dfrac{z^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{16} = 1$ for $z \\geq 2.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola $\\dfrac{z^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{16} = 1$ for $z \\geq 2$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x , z \\rangle = \\big \\langle 4\\tan{t}, \\, 2\\sec{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\n\\qquad t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{sec}^{2}⁡t}{4}-\\frac{16{tan}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y,\\,2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ and $y \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\sec{t}}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Hyperbolic Cylinder"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3596 :topic/title "Parametrizations of Cylinders"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050409 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869 :topic/math-academy-id 408 :topic/title "Calculating the Intersection of Two Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050473 :topic/math-academy-id 3562 :topic/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050975 :topic/math-academy-id 459 :topic/title "The Distance Formula"}] :topic/id #uuid "46beb5e4-992f-546d-8051-796c949740a1" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073810 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073434 :question/id #uuid "2ce03c34-8e2c-5554-a129-1eaf05ad46fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12548" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(3,2)$ and the line $x=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](images/95/952be9b27e5350f755f0f534883f3c70516bb4e03b34e9448ab5d43950d4bad6.png)\n\nSince the line $x = 5$ is vertical, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(3,2)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $x$-coordinate of the point and $5.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd = |3 - 5| = 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073435 :question/id #uuid "64a6782b-aa64-5027-8311-3498c19c39e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12549" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(1,3)$ and the line $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](images/5c/5ccfa3c27c6bb2759ffc37c73b3640583fa962ee79f47b21f02ac0c1d9164b00.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x - 5$ and passes through the point $(1,3).$ \n\nThe given line has a slope of $\\dfrac{1}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-2.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-2(x-1) \\\\ y-3 & =-2x+2 \\\\ y & =-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{2}x-5 \\\\ y=-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by setting the two equations equal and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-5 & =-2x+5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+2x & =5+5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}x & =10 \\\\ x & =10\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-3.$ So, our solution is $x=4$ and $y=-3.$ Consequently, the lines intersect at the point $\\left( 4,-3\\right).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(1,3)$ and $\\left( 4, -3 \\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(4-1)}^{2}+{(-3-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9}\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(1,3)$ and the line $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$ is $3 \\sqrt{5}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073436 :question/id #uuid "57fc631b-c251-55d2-a9d5-6b41fc4bb00c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12612" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c48fc163b3c9543dcfdd03b20882f0d2a19747672c6b1db2a42a683f0014fe25.png)\n\n \n\nFind the point on the line $y-x=4$ that is closest to the point $(3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-x & =4 \\\\ y & =x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y =  x + 4$ and passes through the point $(3,3).$ \n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-1(x-3) \\\\ y-3 & =-x+3 \\\\ y & =-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x+4 \\\\ y=-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =-x+6 \\\\ x+x & =6-4 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=5.$ So, our solution is $x=1$ and $y=5.$ Consequently, the lines intersect at the point $(1,5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y-x= 4$ that is closest to the point $(3,3)$ is  $(1,5).$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :topic/math-academy-id 526 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049110 :topic/math-academy-id 4166 :topic/title "Moments and Centers of Mass of Plane Laminas"}] :topic/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Line"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050748 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048497 :topic/math-academy-id 2119 :topic/title "Factoring Cubic Polynomials Using the Factor Theorem"}] :topic/id #uuid "63760cd7-b8ea-5883-8209-c5979e5bdde0" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054803 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054574 :question/id #uuid "ae9cba85-b0ed-59c9-bf9f-682b921c5ee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6258" :question/problem "Given that $(x-1)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of the polynomial $f(x)= x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6,$ factor $f(x)$ completely." :question/worked-solution "To factor $f(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-7$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6}{x-1} = x^3-7x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 = (x-1) (x^3-7x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^3-7x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-7x-6}{x-3} = x^2+3x +2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 =  (x-1) (x-3) (x^2+3x +2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 =  (x-1) (x-3) (x+2)(x+1).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054804 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054575 :question/id #uuid "7e2e4745-f999-5b5e-bf4b-22b9596c384c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6259" :question/problem "Let $p(x) = x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4.$ Knowing that $p(4) = p(-1) = 0,$ factor the polynomial $p(x)$ completely." :question/worked-solution "Since $p(4) = p(-1) = 0,$ the factor theorem guarantees that $(x - 4)$ and $(x + 1)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x - 4)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x - 4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}4$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-9$ | $-11$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $12$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4}{x-4} = x^3+3x^2+3x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4) (  x^3+3x^2+3x+1).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $( x^3+3x^2+3x+1).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ x^3+3x^2+3x-1}{x+1} = x^2+2x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4)(x+1)(x^2+2x+1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}-11x-4 & =(x-4)(x+1)(x+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Roots"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054805 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054573 :question/id #uuid "99356744-762f-57de-8bc5-9da70ddefa90" :question/math-academy-id "e-6203" :question/problem "Let $f(x) = 3 x^4 - 5 x^3 - 3 x^2 + 6 x - 8.$ If $x=2$ and $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ are zeros of $f(x),$ factor $f(x)$ completely." :question/worked-solution "Because $x=2$ and $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ are both zeros of $f(x),$ the factor theorem guarantees that $(x-2)$ and $\\left( x + \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ are both factors of $f(x).$ So, $(x-2)$ and $\\left( x + \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ both divide $f(x).$\n\nTo begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by one of these factors (it doesn't matter which one we divide first). Let's divide $f(x)$ by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}2$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $6$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $8$ |\n|  | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-8 \\\\  & =(x-2)(3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, because $\\left( x+\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $3x^3 + x^2 - x +4.$ So, we divide this quotient by $\\left( x+\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x+4 & =(x+\\frac{4}{3})(3{x}^{2}-3x+3) \\\\  & =(x+\\frac{4}{3})(3)({x}^{2}-x+1) \\\\  & =(3x+4)({x}^{2}-x+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-8 & =(x-2)(3x+4)({x}^{2}-x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quadratic polynomial $x^2-x+1$ does not appear to factor over the real numbers. We can make sure of this by verifying that its discriminant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={b}^{2}-4ac=(-1)^{2}-4(1)(1)=-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-2)(3x+4)(x^2-x+1).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial With Only Two Real Factors"}] :topic/math-academy-id 104 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049683 :topic/math-academy-id 897 :topic/title "The Fundamental Theorem of Algebra with Quartic Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050767 :topic/math-academy-id 2734 :topic/title "Finding Multiplicities of the Roots of Quartic Polynomials by Factoring"}] :topic/title "Factoring Quartic Polynomials Using the Factor Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644 :topic/math-academy-id 986 :topic/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line Using Differentiation"}] :topic/id #uuid "7fcc3a06-676a-55a2-9979-d3726053babe" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096400 :question/id #uuid "19bf8d06-cbcc-547b-b882-ac89cd39fd1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-181" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^4+x^3)^5,$ calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf = g^5, \\qquad g = 3x^4+x^3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g}  = 5g^4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 12x^3 + 3x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5(3{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}{)}^{4}(12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096401 :question/id #uuid "8fcdb2c8-71ca-5bfa-b7fe-49f8f2c05bbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-182" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{1-x^2},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nHere, we've used $u$ instead of $g$ for the intermediate variable. \n\nLet $u=1-x^2.$ Then $y = \\sqrt u = u^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{2}u^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt u}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}  = -2x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096402 :question/id #uuid "2c37ae73-233d-5e0d-87ff-05cacc6d078b" :question/math-academy-id "e-183" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-3x}},$ calculate $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x)) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{g}} = g^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{-1/2}{)}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x{)}^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2(1-3x{)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1108 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049332 :topic/math-academy-id 1036 :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Logarithmic Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271 :topic/math-academy-id 1007 :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Exponential Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050628 :topic/math-academy-id 334 :topic/title "Integrating Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789 :topic/math-academy-id 305 :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Trigonometric Functions"}] :topic/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation"}]}
